    {
      "block_id": "page-0093-block-0030",
      "order": 30,
      "text": "*Time to peak is not the same as time to reach therapeutic plasma concentration, but both are dependent on dose. \nFor large (loading) doses, therapeutic activity is often seen before attaining peak levels. For low (test) doses, the \ninitial peak level may be sub-therapeutic.\n**Attainment of steady state (SS) follows logarithmic, not linear characteristics: around 90% of SS levels are \nachieved in three half-lives. Time to attain steady state is independent of dose and dosing frequency (i.e. you can’t \nhurry it up by giving more, more often). Loading doses can be used to produce prompt therapeutic plasma levels \nbut time to SS remains the same.\n***Some estimates suggest peak concentrations after only a few hours.24,25 It is likely that fluphenazine decanoate \nproduces two peaks – one on the day of injection and a second slightly higher peak a week or so later.12\n",
      "bbox": [
        61.88890075683594,
        380.82952880859375,
        433.5365905761719,
        469.5320129394531
      ],
      "text_sha256": "c64b4d54df783244e4157f46998168bfdbc86dce3f847f5a435889e87488eb3e"
    },
